> Bash git status --short; /usr/local/bin/repo-looper --repo fleet-seatrials --issue 16 --checkpoint "Stopped for decision (needs-decision): one-place trust domain needs tacplus-dev/operator#14 (opened); options in issue comment. Nothing committed." 2>&1 | tail -2
< remote:  [2026-10-29T02:07:55Z] checkpoint of #16 (a step) pushed to looper/fleet-seatrials-16:  2 files changed, 170 insertions(+)
I stopped issue #16 to ask for a decision. Nothing is committed or pushed to main, and the issue stays open with the `needs-decision` label.

**Why it can't be done in one place yet.** The service configs are generated by the operator this repo pins (`3d0e4b7`), and it reads the trust domain from two different files:
- **Kernel and every service config:** `spiffe_trust_domain` in `deploy/bootstrap/services/kernel.yaml`. We don't set it, so it defaults to `local.dev`.
- **kmaker (the name in kernel's own certificate):** `spiffe_prefix` in `deploy/bootstrap/operator.yaml`, which also defaults to `local.dev`.

The operator's own docs say `spiffe_prefix` is passed on to kernel and every service config, but the code doesn't do that, on the pinned commit or on operator main. So the fleet config can't be the single setting the issue asks for without an operator change, and setting it in each file is what the issue rules out.

**What I did:**
- Opened the operator bug as tacplus-dev/operator#14, with a proposed fix: `spiffe_prefix` becomes the one setting, kernel and the services take it from there, and config generation refuses the two values if they differ.
- Commented on #16 (https://github.com/tacplus-dev/fleet-seatrials/issues/16#issuecomment-6485913284) with two options:
  - **Recommended:** wait for operator#14. Then move this repo's operator pin, set `spiffe_prefix: seatrials.tacplus.dev` in `operator.yaml`, and add a check that every generated config names `seatrials.tacplus.dev`. Moving the pin also waits on #1 (the fleet moving to tacplus 2.4), because operator main now requires 2.4.
  - **Interim:** set the domain in both files now, with the same check making sure they agree. The `kernel.yaml` line goes away once operator#14 lands.
- Saved a checkpoint to `looper/fleet-seatrials-16`.

**For the deploy step with Jody:** Anthropic's sign-in rule for llmproxy (`llmproxy-seatrials`) matches `spiffe://local.dev/system/services/llmproxy`. It has to be changed in the Anthropic Console when the trust domain changes, or llmproxy loses access to Anthropic.

fleet-dev isn't in this repository, so I didn't touch it.
